{
  "image_name": "2ea8062d6d5022415e5af5998eab6d9f.png",
  "prompt_text": "This schematic summarizes how pro-tumour γδ T cells are regulated and how they promote cancer. The central cell is a γδ T cell that can expand and produce IL-17 under the influence of signals from the microenvironment. Commensal bacteria and myeloid cells appear to enhance this program, with cytokines such as IL-1β, IL-6, IL-23 and TGFβ feeding into the γδ T cell. IL-7/STAT3 signaling is shown driving proliferation, while factors including RORγt and NOS2 are linked to IL-17 production.  \n\nThe figure contrasts this with inhibitory influences: neutrophil-derived ROS and dendritic cells are depicted as suppressing IL-17 production.  \n\nThe right side highlights the consequences of γδ T-cell activity. IL-17 mediates several pro-tumour effects, including epithelial transformation or tumour cell proliferation, recruitment of pro-inflammatory macrophages and neutrophils, angiogenesis, and inhibition of CD8+ T-cell responses. The same γδ T cells also have non-IL-17 tumour-promoting actions, secreting amphiregulin and IL-22 to stimulate tumour cell proliferation, and expressing PDL1, galectin-1, galectin-9, and IL-4 to dampen broader T-cell responses. Overall, the figure presents γδ T cells as a hub connecting inflammation, immune suppression, and tumour growth.",
